Burst Balloons (Interval Dynamic Programming)

This is a classic MAANG / hard interview problem.

🎯 Aim

Given an array of balloons, burst them in an optimal order to maximize the total coins collected.

🔍 Problem Statement

Each balloon has a number on it.
When you burst balloon i, the coins gained are:

nums[left] × nums[i] × nums[right]


where left and right are the nearest unburst balloons.

Example
Input: nums = [3, 1, 5, 8]
Output: 167

🧠 Key Trick (VERY IMPORTANT)

Add 1 at both ends:

Original: [3,1,5,8]
Modified: [1,3,1,5,8,1]


Why?

Removes edge-case handling

Simplifies DP transitions

💡 DP Idea (Interval DP)

Instead of deciding which balloon to burst first,
we decide which balloon to burst LAST in a range.

This is the magic insight ✨

⚙️ Algorithm

Pad array with 1 at both ends

Create dp[left][right]

Max coins from bursting balloons between (left, right)

Try every balloon i as last balloon in (left, right)

Formula:

dp[left][right] =
    max(dp[left][i] + dp[i][right] + nums[left]*nums[i]*nums[right])


Return dp[0][n+1]

💻 Java Code (Hard-DP, Interview Ready)
class BurstBalloons {

    public static int maxCoins(int[] nums) {

        int n = nums.length;

        // Step 1: Create new array with padding
        int[] arr = new int[n + 2];
        arr[0] = 1;
        arr[n + 1] = 1;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            arr[i + 1] = nums[i];
        }

        // Step 2: DP table
        int[][] dp = new int[n + 2][n + 2];

        // Step 3: Interval length
        for (int len = 2; len < n + 2; len++) {
            for (int left = 0; left + len < n + 2; left++) {
                int right = left + len;

                // Step 4: Choose last balloon to burst
                for (int i = left + 1; i < right; i++) {
                    int coins = dp[left][i]
                              + dp[i][right]
                              + arr[left] * arr[i] * arr[right];

                    dp[left][right] = Math.max(dp[left][right], coins);
                }
            }
        }

        return dp[0][n + 1];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] nums = {3, 1, 5, 8};
        System.out.println(maxCoins(nums));
    }
}

🧾 Output
167

🪜 Line-by-Line Explanation
🔹 Padding array
arr[0] = 1;
arr[n + 1] = 1;


Ensures multiplication always has valid neighbors.

🔹 DP Table
dp[left][right]


Stores maximum coins for bursting balloons between left and right.

🔹 Interval length loop

We build smaller intervals first → larger ones.

🔹 Core transition
dp[left][i] + dp[i][right] + arr[left]*arr[i]*arr[right]


Interpretation:

Left side solved optimally

Right side solved optimally

i burst last → neighbors fixed

🔍 Dry Run (High Level)

For [3,1,5,8], best order is:

1 → 5 → 3 → 8


Total = 167

⏱️ Complexity

Time: O(n³)

Space: O(n²)
